kill
Usage: kill [-l] [-SIG] PID...

Send a signal (default: TERM) to given PIDs. "kill" is a misnomer: it
sends a signal, and most signals can be caught or ignored by the target.

	-l	List all signal names and numbers
	-SIG	Send SIG instead of TERM, by number or name

The three that matter:

	-15	TERM	ask the process to exit. catchable, so it can
			close files and clean up. this is the default.
	-9	KILL	cannot be caught, blocked or ignored. the kernel
			destroys the process immediately, so it never gets
			to clean up. a last resort, not a first move.
	-1	HUP	historically "hangup"; many daemons reload their
			config on it instead of exiting.

	kill 1234		TERM, the polite default
	kill -9 1234		KILL, unconditional
	kill -HUP 1234		by name instead of number

See also: pkill, pgrep, ps, trap
